<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arnold Schoenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Schoenberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arnold Schoenberg was an Austrian composer who developed atonal music and later the twelve-tone method of composition. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-viennese-school">Second Viennese School</a>, whose members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-berg">Alban Berg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-webern">Anton Webern</a>. He is cited as part of the cultural innovation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in the final decades of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg.jpg" alt="Arnold Schoenberg" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Man Ray · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%98%BF%E8%AF%BA%E5%BE%B7%C2%B7%E5%8B%8B%E4%BC%AF%E6%A0%BC%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arnold Schoenberg was born on 13 September 1874 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>'s Leopoldstadt, into a lower-middle-class Jewish family. His father Samuel, a shoe shopkeeper, came from Szécsény in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and his mother Pauline Nachod was from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> family. He began violin at eight and taught himself to write music by arranging and imitating what he played. Largely self-taught, he began studying counterpoint with Alexander von Zemlinsky about 1894. In 1898 he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, in keeping with the Jewish assimilation of the time, but his Jewish identity remained integral to him amid rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>. In 1901 he married Zemlinsky's sister Mathilde.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his twenties Schoenberg supported himself by orchestrating operettas while composing his own music, among it the string sextet Verklärte Nacht (1899). In 1911 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where his paintings were shown beside those of Kandinsky and Marc in exhibitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/der-blaue-reiter">Der Blaue Reiter</a>. In 1918 he founded the Society for Private Musical Performances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, which gave 353 performances before it went defunct amid the Austrian hyperinflation.</p><p>From 1925 he led the composition master class at the Prussian Academy of Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. When the Nazis took power in 1933 he was warned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and formally returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> synagogue. He reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on 31 October 1933 and adopted the spelling Schoenberg. He taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, where he became visiting professor at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Los Angeles in 1935 and professor in 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His music is traditionally divided into three periods. The first, 1894 to 1907, is Romantic, with traits of both Brahms and Wagner. The second, 1908 to 1922, experiments with extended tonality and the abandonment of key centres; his String Quartet No. 2 was the first piece with no stated key centre, and Pierrot lunaire (1912) belongs here. The third begins in 1923, when he began to use ordered tone rows, a method he described as composing with twelve tones related only to one another. Among his twelve-tone works are the Variations for Orchestra and the unfinished opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> und Aron.</p><p>The Nazis denounced his music as degenerate. After the war he memorialised the victims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> in A Survivor from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> (1947). His pupils included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-berg">Alban Berg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-webern">Anton Webern</a>, and his work influenced Milton Babbitt, Pierre Boulez and Theodor W. Adorno.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arnold Schoenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…intellectual capitals of the Western world, producing the foundations of psychoanalysis (Freud), political Zionism (Herzl), the art of the Secession and Expressionism (Klimt, Schiele, Kokoschka), the Second Viennese School of music (<b>Schoenberg</b>, Berg, Webern), and the Austrian school of economics (Menger), while nurturing thinkers such as Ludwig Wittgenstein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Cubism arose in the studios of Henri Matisse, Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque between 1900 and 1910; in 1907 Picasso painted Les Demoiselles d’Avignon and Oskar Kokoschka wrote the first Expressionist play, and <b>Arnold Schoenberg</b>’s Second String Quartet of 1908 was his first composition without a tonal centre.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>122, later orchestrated by Erich Leinsdorf; <b>Arnold Schoenberg</b> in his Weihnachtsmusik of 1921, which sets it against &quot;Stille Nacht&quot;; Hugo Distler in his oratorio Die Weihnachtsgeschichte of 1933; and Jan Sandström, whose 1990 work for two a cappella choirs incorporates Praetorius's setting in the first choir.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li><li><blockquote>Herman Bellstedt wrote a theme and variations on it, Napoli, and <b>Arnold Schoenberg</b> arranged it for ensemble in 1921.</blockquote><a href="/songs/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1924" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arnold Schoenberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1924">What was here in 1924? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
